"4 × 10 kilometres Relay, Men"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "1889"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "1891"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "1893"} "1932"] "\n" [:option {:value "1901"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "1907"} "1952"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/185"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "18 kilometres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "12 February 1936 — 10:02"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/36"} "Olympia-Skistadion, Garmisch-Partenkirchen"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/1895"} "75 from 22 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "Course Length: ?" [:br {}] "Height Differential: 275 m" [:br {}] "Maximum Climb: ?" [:br {}] "Total Climbing: ?"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Two days after the dramatic " [:a {:href "/results/1897"} "relay competition"] ", a record field of 75 skiers lined up for the 18 km, of which 16 were entered both for the 18 km and the " [:a {:href "/results/2199"} "Nordic Combined"] ". In addition, another 35 skiers competed only in the combined event, making a total field for the day of 110 skiers, starting at 30 second intervals. The conditions were excellent, temperatures varying from -2º to -5º C around the course, which had an elevation change of 275 metres."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The highly favored Scandinavian teams had made minor changes from their relay quartets. Most surprising perhaps, Finland had put " [:a {:href "/athletes/95828"} "Pekka Niemi"] " onto the team instead of " [:a {:href "/athletes/87807"} "Klaes Karppinen"] ", World Champion in the event in 1935. The Norwegians had entered the 1930 World champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/97015"} "Arne Rustadstuen"] " instead of " [:a {:href "/athletes/84376"} "Sverre Brodahl"] ", who had to be content with starting “only” in the Nordic combined. " [:a {:href "/athletes/86368"} "Oddbjørn Hagen"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/86705"} "Olaf Hoffsbakken"] " were entered for both events."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Of the early starters, " [:a {:href "/athletes/95432"} "Lukáš Mihalák"] " from Czechoslovakia was the first skier to beat 1-20 at the finish, and he was in the lead until Norway’s double World champion in Nordic Combined from 1934 and 1935, Oddbjørn Hagen, reached the finishing line in a time almost three and a half minutes faster. Neither Finland’s World Champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/95929"} "Sulo Nurmela"] ", nor the Swede " [:a {:href "/athletes/95234"} "Martin Matsbo"] " could match Hagen, Matsbo finishing 1:29 behind. But the Swede " [:a {:href "/athletes/89438"} "Erik Larsson"] ", nicknamed “Kiruna-Lasse” (he was a miner from Kiruna, in Swedish Lapland), made the race of his life. Starting half a minute behind Rustadstuen, he caught the Norwegian early and pushed ahead mercilessly, beating Hagen’s leading time by 55 seconds. Of the late starters, Niemi was able to beat Matsbo by a scant margin and secure a bronze medal for Finland. The hero from the relay race, " [:a {:href "/athletes/87451"} "Kalle Jalkanen"] ", starting next to last and wearing start number 111, had a bad day and could only finish in a disappointing 12th place, beaten by the Swedish Olympic champion by almost five minutes."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Erik Larsson, only aged 23 when we won his Olympic gold, was born in a poor family, in the Finnish-speaking village of Kurravarra, 12 km north-east of the mining town of Kiruna.  Shortly after his Olympic gold, he ended his sport career, donating his prizes and Olympic medals to his local congregation, and became a leading Laestadian Christian preacher in Kiruna. His granddaughter, Åsa Larsson, became one of Sweden’s most well known crime fiction authors.  Bronze medalist Pekka Niemi, born in Finnish Lapland, had a great year in 1937, when he was 50 km World Champion. In 1938, he also won the prestigious Holmenkollen over the same distance."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button909176", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button909176').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "93"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/89438"} "Erik Larsson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} "1-14:38"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "59"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/86368"} "Oddbjørn Hagen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]] [:td {} "1-15:33"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "104"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/95828"} "Pekka Niemi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FIN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FIN.png"}] "FIN"]] [:td {} "1-16:59"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "78"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/95234"} "Martin Matsbo"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countr